      "text": "For the base, it is a square with each side measuring 10 cm. Therefore, the area of the base is found by squaring one of its sides: (10^2 = 100) square centimeters.\n\nNext, to find the surface area of the pyramid, we consider both the base and its four triangular faces.\n\n1. Each triangular face has a base equal to the length of one side of the square base, which is 10 cm. For the height, it becomes the slant height of each triangular face. To determine the slant height, consider the right triangle formed by the height of the pyramid, half of the base's side length, and the slant height. When taking half of 10 cm (5 cm), it constitutes the adjacent side of the right triangle for a right-angled triangle. Given the peak height of 12 cm represents the hypotenuse, we employ the Pythagorean theorem to find the slant height (representing the opposite side):\n\n[\ntext{opposite}^2 + 5^2 = 12^2\n]\n\n[\ntext{opposite}^2 = 12^2 - 5^2\n]\n\n[\ntext{opposite} = sqrt{12^2 - 5^2}\n]\n\nTherefore, the area of one triangular face is:\n\n[\na text{side length} times a text{slant height} = 10 times sqrt{12^2 - 5^2}\n]\n\nFinally, since there are four triangular faces, the total area of the pyramid's four triangular faces is:\n\n[\n4 times (10 times sqrt{12^2 - 5^2})\n]\n\nThe total combined area of the pyramid, including both the base and the four triangular faces, then becomes:\n\n[\ntext{Total Area} = (100 + 4 times (10 times sqrt{12^2 - 5^2}))\n]\n\nThus, the total surface area of the given pyramid is:\n\n[\ntext{Total Area}\n]\n\nThis concludes the calculations for determining the total surface area of the pyramid. The answer is (text{Total Area}) square centimeters.",
